    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d33">

                <pb facs="0108" n="66" xml:id="pg66"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 33</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00/11–2151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Strasbourg (</hi>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Andrews</hi><hi rendition="#italic">) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d33-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Strasbourg</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-11-21">November 21,
                        1951—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>53. Notwithstanding apparently successful beginning meeting between Congressional
                    and Assembly delegates reported Contel 51<anchor n="2" xml:id="d33-f2"/> to Dept
                    rptd 10 to Paris, London, Frankfort unn, several jarring notes have been struck
                    by Congressional delegates. At private meeting Congressional del, just after
                    arrival 18th Benton passed around so-called confidential document obtained “not
                    through State Dept” purporting to give designs CE delegation on US involving
                    further monetary contributions by US to Eur and expressed opinion that present
                    affair was mainly propaganda stunt by CE. As result Keating questioned
                    advisability accepting hospitality CE Secretariat in paying for hotel rooms but
                    Cox set matter aright. In public morning session 20th Benton made excuses for
                    inadequacy US dep resulting from alleged lack of briefing, prepared speeches and
                    staff as contrasted with CE and also emphasized point that people in US are
                    “distressed and perplexed” at lack of progress in Eur unity. Cox stressed “fact”
                    that Amer delegation occupied “position more or less as observers and not, as I
                    take it, full participants in meeting.”</p>
                <p>At lunch my house Judd said in presence Spaak and Layton that most of Amer del
                    were unfamiliar with Eur and international affairs and that only two or three
                    were competent reps for present debates and that several were isolationists at
                    heart.</p>
                <p>Although impatience US del over failure some form Eur union by now clearly
                    indicated in most US speeches, no resentment thus far observed on part Eurs,
                    some of whom have in fact remarked that Amers not blunt enough. However, Eur
                    apologia expounded by Spaak and Layton at todays sitting.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Andrews</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d33-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d33-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to all NATO missions except Ottawa and Lisbon, and to Bonn and Vienna.
                    Pouched to missions in other countries who were members of the Council of
                    Europe.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d33-f2" n="2" xml:id="d33-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
